  "question": {
    "text": "The measured value of the length of a simple pendulum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo> </mo><mi>mm</mi></math> accuracy. The time for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>50</mn></math> oscillations was measured to be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn></math> seconds with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math> second resolution. From these measurements, the accuracy in the measurement of acceleration due to gravity is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>N</mi><mo>%</mo></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>N</mi></math> is:",
    "image": null
  },
